  "member_id": "pearce-mark",
  "model": "gpt-6-luna",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_luna_round1_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B026",
        "laureates": [
          "Francis Halzen"
        ],
        "citation": "For opening the field of high-energy neutrino astronomy."
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B003",
    "B002"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "High-energy astrophysical neutrinos give us a new way to observe the universe, sensitive to sources and processes that electromagnetic telescopes cannot reach. IceCube established an astrophysical signal through multiple analyses and growing event samples. Halzen's role as a leading architect and advocate makes him a plausible individual laureate for this opening of a new observational field.",
    "case_against": "The result rests on a large international collaboration and a demanding detector effort, so sole attribution to Halzen needs careful examination. The signal is established, but its sources are not yet fully identified; that limits how completely we understand what the new window has revealed. These are serious reservations, though I do not think incomplete source identification erases the achievement of opening the field.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson argues that optical-lattice platforms are established, while their reach as predictive simulators for difficult physical systems is still developing.",
        "response": "I agree that a platform's promise should not be confused with demonstrated scientific reach. For B026, the case I find mature enough to recognize is the establishment of an astrophysical neutrino signal, not the eventual identification of every source. The unresolved sources remain a real limitation, but the observed signal is itself a distinct opening of an observational window."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "Danielsson makes a strong case for the Higgs discovery as a confirmed result that completed a central prediction, while noting that leadership credit across ATLAS and CMS needs careful assessment.",
        "response": "I agree on both the scientific importance and the attribution difficulty. The Higgs discovery is an exceptionally strong alternative, but a prize to three leaders must still explain how it recognizes two collaborations and thousands of contributors. B026 has a similar collective-credit problem, and Halzen's proposed individual recognition also requires scrutiny; I favor it because the opening of neutrino astronomy is a particularly clear, enduring achievement."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether Halzen's contribution can be distinguished as decisive enough for individual recognition within IceCube's large international collaboration.",
      "How much the still-incomplete identification of astrophysical neutrino sources should affect the maturity of the achievement."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No direct connection to Halzen or IceCube is apparent from the supplied profile. My work in astroparticle physics is in an adjacent field, which I disclose."
  }
}
